.TH PATHCHK 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
pathchk \- check pathnames
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pathchk
.I pathname
.RI [ pathname ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pathchk
checks each
.I pathname
operand against the limits and naming rules of the underlying
filesystem, without creating, opening, or otherwise touching the file
it names. Multiple operands are processed in turn; a failed check on
one operand does not stop the rest from being checked. Nothing is
written to standard output; results are reported only through
diagnostics on standard error and the exit status.
.PP
For each
.IR pathname \|,
a diagnostic is written, and the exit status is set to reflect
failure, if any of the following is true:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
The whole
.I pathname
is longer than the filesystem's maximum pathname length.
.IP \(bu 2
Any single component of
.I pathname
is longer than the filesystem's maximum component (file name) length.
.IP \(bu 2
Any component of
.I pathname
contains a byte that is not valid in the directory that contains it:
on this build, that means any ASCII control byte (0x00\-0x1F), or any
of the seven characters
.BR < ", " > ", " : ,
a double quote,
.BR | ", " ? ", and " * .
.IP \(bu 2
An existing directory named by a leading portion of
.I pathname
is not searchable by the invoking process.
.RE
.PP
A leading portion of
.I pathname
that does not exist at all is not treated as an error by itself \(em
checking of the search-permission condition above simply stops at the
first pathname prefix that does not exist, since there is nothing left
to check the permissions of.
.PP
A leading drive letter and colon (such as
.BR C: )
are recognized and are not themselves treated as a pathname component
subject to the component-length or searchability checks.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-P
Not implemented. Each causes
.B pathchk
to print a diagnostic and exit immediately with status 2 rather than
run a different check set than the default one described above:
.B \-p
would check against fixed, portable minimum limits instead of this
filesystem's real ones, and
.B \-P
would additionally forbid a leading
.B \-
or an empty
.IR pathname \|;
neither substituted check set is implemented.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I pathname
A pathname to check. A single
.B \-
is treated as an ordinary
.I pathname
operand, not as an option.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I pathname
operand passed all of the checks.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred: an invalid or unimplemented option, no
.I pathname
operand at all, or one or more
.I pathname
operands failed a check.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR basename (1),
.BR dirname (1),
.BR realpath (1)
